And you can listen and look at those languages 
and guess what languages they are.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.600
 

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.380
But don't worry even if you don't guess the language 
correctly, it will still be interesting

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.820
as long as you share the reasons 
for your guesses in the comments down below

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.180
So let's give it a try.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.200
Here's the first language.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:42.900
Please, listen:

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.660
Okay and let me also show you a little bit
of that language in its written form.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.520
I don't speak this language 
but I'll try to read it as best I can.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.000
So what language is it?

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.860
The language is Haitian Creole.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.680
If you're a French speaker, then right away you might 
have known that this was a Haitian Creole.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.520
And other people, you might have noticed some words that sounded French but sounded a little different

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.660
That's because the Haitian Creole language 
is a French-based creole language.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:51.420
It arose in the 17th and 18th centuries because of
interaction between French settlers and african slaves 
in the area that is now called Haiti.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.080
The majority of its vocabulary derives from French.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:59.520
But the majority of its syntax derives from
the African languages spoken by the slaves.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.180
Let's go back to those two example sentences
for a second.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.660
This means 
"is there a person here who speaks English?"

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.460
The beginning of the sentence 
doesn't really seem French.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.440
"Gen" means "to have" or "there is". 
It might derive from the French word "gagner"

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.760
But the way it's used is quite different
from the French word.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.200
And "moun" means "person" 
and I don't know the origin of this word.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.740
"isit ki palé Anglé" is like
"ici qui parle Anglais" in French
(= "here who speaks English")

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.820
In French, the whole sentence, I guess, would be:
"Il y a quelqu'un ici qui parle anglais"
(Rem : rather "Y a-t-il ici quelqu'un qui parle anglais?")

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.120
The second sentence

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.020
This means "I can't speak English".

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.320
"Mwen" comes from "moi" in French.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.920
"pa" is the French negation marker "pas"

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.420
"kap" comes from the French "capable".

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.280
"palé" is "parler"

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:48.400
So the vocabulary is all kind of French 
but in French it would be like this :

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.540
So obviously, the grammar is very different, 
even though the vocabulary is French-based.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.480
Notice that in the French sentence, 
"moi" is not used but "je" is used

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.840
That's because, in French, "moi" is the object pronoun,
while "je" is the subject pronoun.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.180
In Haitian Creole, "mwen" is both.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.740
Now, I'm breaking down these sentences just 
to show you that Haitian Creole is a combination 
of French and other influences.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.580
To learn more about Creole languages, check out 
my video on Pidgins and Creoles right here.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.380
Ok, on to the next language.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:18.240
Please, listen:

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.400
And again, let me show you a little bit 
of that language in its written form.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.560
So, what language is it?

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:11.700
The language is Frisian, specifically West Frisian.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.640
West Frisian is spoken in a northern province 
of the Netherlands called Friesland.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:20.780
Frisian is thought to be the language 
that is most closely related to English.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.880
Now, it doesn't mean that it's easy 
for English speakers to understand Frisian.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.940
When I hear it or when I try to read it, 
I barely understand anything.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.860
But it is closely related to English in its historical 
development and in the structure of the language.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.820
Alright, let's do one last language.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:34.560
Please, listen.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.000
And again, let's look at a couple of phrases visually.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.220
Now, I don't speak this language 
but I'll try to read this as best I can.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:04.900
So what language is it?

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:14.240
Well, if you know a Romance language,
then you probably noticed right away that 
this is definitely another Romance language

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:18.860
It is actually the Sardinian language, 
which is spoken on the island of Sardinia in Italy.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:25.360
It is one of many regional languages in Italy 
that are often mistakenly referred to as dialects 
but they're actually separate languages.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:30.120
Most of them are closely related to Italian but they are
different enough to be considered different languages.

